<main id="top" class="subpage-main">
    <section class="page-header"><div class="container">
        <h1>Generator installation in Ann Arbor</h1>
        <p class="section-subheading">Home energy resilience</p>
        <p>Keep the things you depend on running when the power goes out. Tell us what matters most: heat, the sump pump, the fridge, or more of the house.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/get-help.html?service=generator">Tell us what you want to power →</a>
    </div></section>
    <figure class="service-banner"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/images/responsive/generator-480.webp 480w, /images/responsive/generator-960.webp 960w, /images/responsive/generator-1440.webp 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1200px) calc(100vw - 56px), 1100px"><img src="/images/generator.jpg" alt="Illustration of a home standby generator" width="1536" height="1024" fetchpriority="high"></picture><figcaption>Illustrative image</figcaption></figure>
    <section class="pricing-detail" id="pricing"><div class="container">
        <h2>What will the installation cost?</h2>
        <p>A connection for a portable generator usually costs less than an automatic standby system. What you want to run, where the generator can go, the wiring route, and the fuel supply determine the work.</p>
        <p>Already own a generator? Tell us the model. If you're starting from scratch, we'll help you compare the options.</p>
        <a class="button dark" href="/get-help.html?service=generator">Start your generator request →</a>
    </div></section>
    <section class="content-section"><div class="container"><div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Choose how you want backup power to work</h2>
        <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
                <div><h3>Automatic standby generator</h3><p>The system starts and transfers power automatically, including when you're away.</p></div>
                <div><h4>What to expect</h4><ul><li>Permanent installation outside your home</li><li>Natural gas or propane fuel</li><li>Capacity for the loads we include in the design</li><li>Routine maintenance and checks during long outages</li></ul></div>
                <div><button type="button" data-generator-target="standby" aria-controls="standby-tab">See standby details ↓</button><a href="generators/standby.html" class="btn btn-secondary">Standby installation →</a></div>
            </div>
            <div class="comparison-card">
                <div><h3>Portable generator connection</h3><p>You set up the generator and switch over manually. An interlock keeps utility and generator power separate.</p></div>
                <div><h4>What to expect</h4><ul><li>An outdoor inlet and compatible panel interlock</li><li>A generator you own or one we help you choose</li><li>Manual setup and a safe outdoor running location</li><li>Limits on how much equipment you can run together</li></ul></div>
                <div><button type="button" data-generator-target="portable" aria-controls="portable-tab">See portable details ↓</button><a href="generators/portable-interlock.html" class="btn btn-secondary">Portable generator hookups →</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="tab-container">
            <div class="tab-buttons" role="tablist" aria-label="Generator options">
                <button type="button" id="standby-tab-button" class="tab-button active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="standby-tab" tabindex="0" data-generator-target="standby">Standby generators</button>
                <button type="button" id="portable-tab-button" class="tab-button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="portable-tab" tabindex="-1" data-generator-target="portable">Portable + interlock kit</button>
            </div>
            <div id="standby-tab" class="tab-content active" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="standby-tab-button" tabindex="0">
                <h2>Backup power that starts automatically</h2>
                <p>A standby system detects an outage, starts the generator, and transfers the connected loads after it stabilizes. When utility power returns, it switches back.</p>
                <p>That helps if you're away from home or don't want to wheel out a generator in bad weather. There is a brief interruption during the transfer. Equipment that needs uninterrupted power needs its own backup plan.</p>
                <h3>Size it around what you need</h3>
                <p>We start with the appliances and systems you want to run. Motors, well pumps, and air conditioners can need extra power to start. Square footage alone won't tell us which generator to install.</p>
                <p>We can plan for essential circuits or broader home backup. Load management can keep selected large appliances from running together when necessary.</p>
                <h3>Fuel and maintenance</h3>
                <p>Natural gas avoids tank refills while the supply remains available. Propane needs enough storage and a plan for refilling. We check the fuel requirements for the selected generator and site.</p>
                <p>Both options need maintenance. Long outages still require shutdowns and checks according to the generator's manual. A gas connection doesn't mean the generator can run unattended indefinitely.</p>
                <p><a href="generators/standby.html">See what a standby installation involves →</a></p>
            </div>
            <div id="portable-tab" class="tab-content" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="portable-tab-button" tabindex="0" hidden>
                <h2>Use a portable generator with your panel</h2>
                <p>We install an outdoor inlet and a compatible interlock so you can connect a suitable portable generator. The interlock prevents the main breaker and generator breaker from turning on together.</p>
                <p>You choose which circuits to run within the generator's capacity. A 50-amp connection doesn't mean you can run every light, outlet, and appliance at once.</p>
                <h3>We check the whole setup</h3>
                <ul><li>Your panel and the interlock that fits it</li><li>The generator's running output, connections, and neutral arrangement</li><li>Which appliances need power and how much they draw</li><li>A safe place to operate the generator and the route to the inlet</li></ul>
                <h3>Gasoline, propane, or natural gas?</h3>
                <p>Some portable generators accept all three. Output and runtime vary by fuel and model. We can discuss a compatible generator and coordinate a natural gas connection with a gas contractor when the site allows it.</p>
                <div class="content-callout warning-box"><h3>Portable generators stay outside</h3><p>Run a portable generator at least 20 feet from the house, with its exhaust pointing away from buildings and openings. Never run it in a garage or shed. Follow its manual and keep working carbon monoxide alarms in the home.</p></div>
                <p><a href="generators/portable-interlock.html">See what a portable generator hookup involves →</a></p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="action-panel"><h2>Not sure which option fits?</h2><p>Tell us what you want to keep running and whether you already have a generator. We'll follow up to discuss the job and arrange a walkthrough.</p><a href="/get-help.html?service=generator" class="btn btn-primary">Tell us about your home</a></div>
        <p>Bremray is a Generac Aligned Partner. We install generators in Ann Arbor and Washtenaw County.</p>
    </div></div></section>
    <aside class="page-references" aria-label="Generator information sources"><div class="container"><details><summary>Generator information sources</summary><p><a href="https://www.generac.com/resources/home-management/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">Generac: generator sizing</a>; <a href="https://support.generac.com/s/article/Continuous-use-maintenance-for-home-standby-generators">Generac: maintenance during long outages</a>; <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/Newsroom/News-Releases/2022/CPSC-Releases-New-Report-on-Carbon-Monoxide-CO-Fatalities-Urges-Generator-Safety-in-New-PSA">CPSC: portable generator safety</a>.</p></details></div></aside>
</main>
